    title: "Lithography Basics",
    date: "2026-08-07",
    body: `The core idea

A modern chip starts as a flat silicon wafer. To turn that blank wafer into billions of transistors, manufacturers repeatedly create incredibly tiny patterns on its surface.

Lithography is the step that prints those patterns.

Think of it like an extremely advanced projector: light shines through a pattern and transfers that pattern onto a light-sensitive chemical coating on the wafer. The smaller and more precise the pattern, the more transistors can fit on a chip.

Why lithography matters so much

Smaller transistors generally allow chips to pack in more computing power and improve energy efficiency. For advanced AI chips such as NVIDIA GPUs, manufacturing leading-edge transistors requires some of the most sophisticated lithography equipment ever built.

This is why ASML is one of the most strategically important companies in the AI supply chain.

The two main technologies

DUV — Deep Ultraviolet lithography

- Older and still extremely important.

- Used across many layers of advanced chips and for huge volumes of mature-node chips.

- Main suppliers: ASML, Nikon and Canon.

EUV — Extreme Ultraviolet lithography

- Uses light with a much shorter wavelength, allowing much smaller features to be printed.

- Essential for economically manufacturing today’s most advanced logic chips.

- ASML is the only company in the world that sells EUV lithography machines.

That monopoly is the first major bottleneck to remember in the AI supply chain.

ASML does not build everything itself

An EUV machine is really a system built from an enormous supplier network.

Three names to know:

ZEISS — makes the ultra-precise mirrors and optical systems. Normal lenses cannot handle EUV light, so the machine uses mirrors instead.

TRUMPF — supplies extremely powerful laser technology. The laser helps create the EUV light source.

Cymer — a light-source specialist acquired by ASML. It is a critical part of ASML’s lithography technology base.

Later lessons will go several layers deeper into each of these suppliers and their own supply chains.

How EUV light is created — simplified

This is one of the strangest parts of semiconductor manufacturing.

- Tiny droplets of tin are fired through the machine.

- A powerful laser hits each droplet.

- The tin becomes an extremely hot plasma.

- That plasma emits EUV light.

- Mirrors guide the light through the machine and eventually onto the wafer.

All of this happens thousands of times per second with extraordinary precision.

The key takeaway is not to memorize the physics yet. It is to understand why recreating ASML is so difficult: the machine requires world-class expertise in lasers, optics, plasma physics, precision motion, vacuum systems, software and semiconductor manufacturing simultaneously.

Economics

A leading EUV machine costs roughly hundreds of millions of dollars. The newest High-NA EUV systems are even more expensive.

Customers include leading-edge chip manufacturers such as:

- TSMC

- Samsung Electronics

- Intel

These companies do not buy one machine and start producing chips immediately. A leading-edge fab requires many pieces of equipment from dozens of suppliers, extensive installation and qualification, and billions to tens of billions of dollars of total investment.

Is lithography cyclical?

Yes — but less simply than many semiconductor businesses.

Chip-equipment spending rises and falls with semiconductor demand, customer capex plans and new fab construction. Memory equipment can be particularly cyclical.

ASML has some protection because:

- it is the sole EUV supplier;

- leading-edge chipmakers cannot easily substitute another machine;

- each new process generation tends to require more sophisticated lithography;

- AI is pushing customers to build more leading-edge capacity.

But ASML is not immune to cycles. TSMC, Samsung or Intel delaying factories can push ASML revenue out in time, and export restrictions can affect where machines can be sold.

Investment-banking way to think about it

Imagine a massive industry where every company wants to build better factories, but one supplier owns the only machine capable of performing a critical step at the technological frontier.

That supplier has unusual pricing power and strategic value.
